Use ASP.NET Core's **`WebApplicationFactory<T>`** — it hosts your app in-process with an in-memory test server. You send real HTTP requests through `HttpClient` but nothing binds to a port.

**Setup (xUnit example):**

```csharp
// Install: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing, xunit

public class McpTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public McpTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        _client = factory.CreateClient();
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task Initialize_ReturnsCapabilities()
    {
        var request = new
        {
            jsonrpc = "2.0",
            id = 1,
            method = "initialize",
            @params = new
            {
                protocolVersion = "2025-03-26",
                capabilities = new { },
                clientInfo = new { name = "test", version = "1.0" }
            }
        };

        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);

        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        var body = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
        Assert.Equal("2025-03-26",
            body.GetProperty("result")
                .GetProperty("protocolVersion")
                .GetString());
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task ToolsCall_ExecutesTool()
    {
        // Initialize first (stateful session may require it)
        await InitializeSession();

        var request = new
        {
            jsonrpc = "2.0",
            id = 2,
            method = "tools/call",
            @params = new
            {
                name = "my-tool",
                arguments = new { input = "hello" }
            }
        };

        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);

        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        var body = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
        var content = body.GetProperty("result")
                         .GetProperty("content")[0];
        Assert.Equal("text", content.GetProperty("type").GetString());
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **`WebApplicationFactory<Program>`** boots your real `Program.cs` pipeline (middleware, DI, routing) in-memory — no port, no process.
- Override services for isolation (mock DBs, etc.) via `factory.WithWebHostBuilder(b => b.ConfigureServices(...))`.
- If your server uses **SSE** (`text/event-stream`), read the response stream incrementally:

```csharp
using var stream = await response.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
using var reader = new StreamReader(stream);
var line = await reader.ReadLineAsync(); // each SSE event
```

- If your server uses the **Streamable HTTP** transport with session headers (`Mcp-Session-Id`), capture and resend the header across requests.
- Make `Program` visible to the test project by adding to your server's `Program.cs`:

```csharp
// Bottom of Program.cs (or use InternalsVisibleTo)
public partial class Program { }
```

This gives you full protocol-level coverage with real HTTP semantics, zero network overhead, and no port conflicts in CI.
